WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.013
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Proficiency.

2
00:00:05.313 --> 00:00:08.143
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.443 --> 00:00:17.469
Unit fifty-three: Diplomacy and Small States. In this unit, you will analyse how small states protect their interests.

4
00:00:18.483 --> 00:00:20.286
small-state diplomacy.

5
00:00:20.986 --> 00:00:27.304
small-state diplomacy means the strategies smaller countries use to influence larger powers.

6
00:00:27.604 --> 00:00:31.275
Singapore is famous for skilful small-state diplomacy.

7
00:00:31.975 --> 00:00:35.974
Your turn. Say: small-state diplomacy.

8
00:00:38.674 --> 00:00:40.384
small-state diplomacy.

9
00:00:41.383 --> 00:00:42.791
hedging strategy.

10
00:00:43.491 --> 00:00:49.147
hedging strategy means keeping good relations with several rival powers to avoid depending on one.

11
00:00:49.447 --> 00:00:54.106
Many ASEAN members follow a hedging strategy between China and the US.

12
00:00:54.806 --> 00:00:58.039
Your turn. Say: hedging strategy.

13
00:01:00.739 --> 00:01:02.065
hedging strategy.

14
00:01:03.050 --> 00:01:04.272
non-alignment.

15
00:01:04.972 --> 00:01:09.804
non-alignment means refusing to join either side in a great-power rivalry.

16
00:01:10.104 --> 00:01:15.842
Cambodia was a founding member of the Non-Aligned Movement in 1961.

17
00:01:16.542 --> 00:01:19.717
Your turn. Say: non-alignment.

18
00:01:22.417 --> 00:01:23.476
non-alignment.

19
00:01:24.483 --> 00:01:26.274
neutrality policy.

20
00:01:26.974 --> 00:01:32.236
neutrality policy means an official position of not taking sides in conflicts.

21
00:01:32.536 --> 00:01:36.021
Switzerland's neutrality policy dates back centuries.

22
00:01:36.721 --> 00:01:40.349
Your turn. Say: neutrality policy.

23
00:01:43.049 --> 00:01:44.596
neutrality policy.

24
00:01:45.583 --> 00:01:47.409
regional integration.

25
00:01:48.109 --> 00:01:55.391
regional integration means countries in a region cooperating closely on trade and policy.

26
00:01:55.691 --> 00:01:59.258
Regional integration gives small economies a bigger market.

27
00:01:59.958 --> 00:02:03.400
Your turn. Say: regional integration.

28
00:02:06.100 --> 00:02:07.728
regional integration.

29
00:02:08.717 --> 00:02:09.938
ASEAN way.

30
00:02:10.638 --> 00:02:17.143
ASEAN way means ASEAN's style of quiet consultation, consensus and non-interference.

31
00:02:17.443 --> 00:02:20.696
The ASEAN way avoids public criticism of members.

32
00:02:21.396 --> 00:02:24.513
Your turn. Say: ASEAN way.

33
00:02:27.213 --> 00:02:28.295
ASEAN way.

34
00:02:29.283 --> 00:02:30.958
consensus diplomacy.

35
00:02:31.658 --> 00:02:36.246
consensus diplomacy means making decisions only when all parties agree.

36
00:02:36.546 --> 00:02:40.345
Consensus diplomacy is slow but keeps everyone at the table.

37
00:02:41.045 --> 00:02:44.650
Your turn. Say: consensus diplomacy.

38
00:02:47.350 --> 00:02:49.117
consensus diplomacy.

39
00:02:50.117 --> 00:02:52.128
strategic autonomy.

40
00:02:52.828 --> 00:02:58.543
strategic autonomy means a country's ability to make independent foreign-policy choices.

41
00:02:58.843 --> 00:03:02.909
Strategic autonomy is the goal of every small state.

42
00:03:03.609 --> 00:03:07.167
Your turn. Say: strategic autonomy.

43
00:03:09.867 --> 00:03:11.866
strategic autonomy.

44
00:03:12.850 --> 00:03:14.977
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:03:15.277 --> 00:03:19.494
Ms. Sarah, how can a small country deal with superpowers?

46
00:03:20.783 --> 00:03:22.983
Carefully. Very carefully.

47
00:03:24.283 --> 00:03:25.935
Should it choose a side?

48
00:03:27.250 --> 00:03:31.237
Some do; most try not to. Hedging, they call it.

49
00:03:32.550 --> 00:03:36.755
Cambodia chose non-alignment in the 1960s.

50
00:03:38.050 --> 00:03:41.759
It did. That balance, Prince Sihanouk tried hard to keep.

51
00:03:43.050 --> 00:03:44.632
And ASEAN helps?

52
00:03:45.917 --> 00:03:52.064
Together, small states are heard; alone, rarely. Hence the value of consensus diplomacy.

53
00:03:53.350 --> 00:03:58.577
Grammar focus: Ellipsis, substitution and fronting (advanced).

54
00:03:58.877 --> 00:04:02.885
Ellipsis and fronting create natural, emphatic speech.

55
00:04:03.185 --> 00:04:07.858
Should small states choose a side? Some do; most try not to.

56
00:04:08.158 --> 00:04:11.272
That balance, Prince Sihanouk tried hard to keep.

57
00:04:11.572 --> 00:04:18.413
Ellipsis and substitution make dialogue natural; fronting highlights the topic you want listeners to remember.

58
00:04:19.417 --> 00:04:22.626
Now read and listen. The Art of Being Small.

59
00:04:22.926 --> 00:04:28.153
Small states face a permanent dilemma: how to remain independent among giants.

60
00:04:29.450 --> 00:04:36.848
Small-state diplomacy relies on skill, reputation and partnerships rather than military power.

61
00:04:38.150 --> 00:04:44.573
During the Cold War, many newly independent countries, Cambodia among them, chose non-alignment.

62
00:04:45.883 --> 00:04:50.774
Others, such as Switzerland, adopted a formal neutrality policy.

63
00:04:52.083 --> 00:04:59.180
Today, many Southeast Asian states pursue a hedging strategy, cooperating with several powers at once.

64
00:05:00.483 --> 00:05:04.758
Regional integration through ASEAN amplifies their collective voice.

65
00:05:06.050 --> 00:05:16.130
The ASEAN way, based on consensus diplomacy and non-interference, is often criticised as slow; supporters reply that it has kept the peace.

66
00:05:17.417 --> 00:05:23.874
For small states, strategic autonomy is not a luxury but a condition of survival.

67
00:05:25.183 --> 00:05:27.754
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:28.054 --> 00:05:31.447
Keeping good relations with several rival powers is a blank.

69
00:05:31.747 --> 00:05:38.303
A, hedging strategy. B, neutrality policy. C, regional integration.

70
00:05:42.317 --> 00:05:45.280
The answer is A: hedging strategy.

71
00:05:46.783 --> 00:05:51.830
'Did Cambodia join? It did.' The words 'It did' are an example of blank.

72
00:05:52.130 --> 00:05:56.863
A, substitution. B, inversion. C, passive voice.

73
00:06:00.850 --> 00:06:03.175
The answer is A: substitution.

74
00:06:04.683 --> 00:06:07.809
'That balance, he tried to keep' fronts the blank.

75
00:06:08.109 --> 00:06:12.482
A, object. B, subject. C, verb.

76
00:06:16.483 --> 00:06:18.738
The answer is A: object.

77
00:06:20.250 --> 00:06:24.235
ASEAN's style of quiet consultation and consensus is the blank.

78
00:06:24.535 --> 00:06:30.371
A, ASEAN way. B, non-alignment. C, strategic autonomy.

79
00:06:34.383 --> 00:06:37.358
The answer is A: ASEAN way.

80
00:06:38.850 --> 00:06:43.253
According to the text, what do supporters say the ASEAN way has done?

81
00:06:43.553 --> 00:06:49.853
A, kept the peace. B, ended all disputes. C, created a single currency.

82
00:06:53.850 --> 00:06:56.268
The answer is A: kept the peace.

83
00:06:57.783 --> 00:07:04.255
Now it's your turn. Part one: How should Cambodia balance relations with major world powers?

84
00:07:09.555 --> 00:07:16.083
Part two: Use one elliptical answer, one substitution and one fronted phrase.

85
00:07:22.383 --> 00:07:34.526
Should Cambodia depend on one partner? I'd say not. Strategic autonomy, that is what we need. Friendly relations with many powers, strong ASEAN ties and careful hedging will keep us independent.

86
00:07:34.826 --> 00:07:37.092
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:38.083 --> 00:07:44.300
Great work today! You learned eight new words and ellipsis, substitution and fronting (advanced).

88
00:07:44.600 --> 00:07:49.284
Today's value is love of country. Love and take care of Cambodia.

89
00:07:49.584 --> 00:07:52.643
See you in Unit fifty-four. Goodbye!

90
00:07:52.943 --> 00:07:56.164
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

